# One important thing about being a developer in 2026

I'd combine books with three other learning resources:

- MDN Web Docs: For current HTML, CSS and JavaScript documentation.
- web.dev: For performance, accessibility and modern web practices.
- Figma: For learning how professional interfaces are designed and translated into working websites.

Books teach you principles. Documentation teaches you current implementation. Projects teach you how to put everything together.

And I would make one rule for myself: For every book I read, I'd build at least one real project using what I learned.

For example:

- After Refactoring UI, redesign an existing website.
- After Eloquent JavaScript, build an interactive application.
- After Don't Make Me Think, audit a website's usability.
- After CSS in Depth, build a responsive landing page from scratch.

That way, you're not just becoming someone who has read 15 books. You're becoming someone who can actually build things.

